cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this repository, cite it as below, together with the access date. The scope note is
  part of the citation: this tool reports state transitions only.
title: >-
  sim-sentinel: a one-minute Windows sentinel that reads a simulation solver's own engine log and
  reports the transition to finished, failed or stalled
abstract: >-
  A one-minute Windows scheduled task plus a file-only state machine. It reads the engine log a
  simulation solver writes itself (`<project>_p0.log`), raises one Chinese toast the first time a
  run is seen to finish, fail or stall, and appends one JSON line per verdict as evidence.


  Three properties are what make the verdicts usable: evidence only (no log, no verdict), transitions
  only (one notification per run regardless of polling rate), and evidence of execution rather than
  proof by echo (heartbeat timestamp, product modification time, append-only event record).


  SCOPE -- this is part of the citation. The tool never queries the solver and does not judge whether
  a result is converged, energy-conserving or correct; those remain with the solver's own criteria
  and the user's review. It covers Windows only, and local engine logs only: a cloud job needs a
  separate read-only poller that never submits or cancels a task. No scheduled task is registered by
  this repository, and nothing here states that any machine is running it. The readings recorded in
  README (rule cases 6/6, a replay of 77 historical logs, 503 logs per poll, 13 consecutive heartbeat
  minutes, 7/7 end-to-end assertions) each carry their own date and machine.
authors:
